=== 3. TYPED COMPARISON, NOT STRING COMPARISON — PASS ===

Source of the data: internal/models/user_profile.go:70-78 —
`Size int64`, `Transactions int`, `MinDate string`, `MaxDate string`.
internal/services/dataloader/loader.go:677-724 fills MinDate/MaxDate with
`minD.Format("2006-01-02")` and leaves them "" when the scan finds no dates.
So data-size is raw bytes, data-rows a raw int, data-mindate ISO or empty.

Comparator read at web/templates/pages/filemanager.html:485-505 (`cmp`):
  - name  -> localeCompare(..., {sensitivity:'base', numeric:true})
  - rows / size / enabled -> `(parseInt(av,10)||0) - (parseInt(bv,10)||0)`
  - mindate -> ""  mapped to Infinity, so empty sorts LAST ascending / FIRST
    descending; otherwise ISO strings compared with `<` (lexical == chronological
    for YYYY-MM-DD).
It reads `a.getAttribute('data-'+column)` only — never cell text.

Verified in a real browser against the running server (fixtures in a scratchpad
data dir, never in the repo). Emitted attributes, initial page:
    alpha-card.csv  rows=1   size=48    mindate="2025-03-01" en=1  cell="alpha-card.csv (0K)"
    big25k.csv      rows=700 size=16714 mindate="2022-01-01" en=0  cell="big25k.csv (16K)"
    mid-nodate.csv  rows=1   size=33    mindate=""           en=0
    uploaded.csv    rows=1   size=50    mindate="2023-06-01" en=0
    zeta-bank.csv   rows=2   size=76    mindate="2024-01-15" en=1
Note the rendered File cell shows "(0K)"/"(16K)" while data-size carries 48 /
16714 — the raw-vs-rendered split the brief demanded.

  mindate ascending  -> 2022-01-01, 2023-06-01, 2024-01-15, 2025-03-01, ""   (empty LAST)
  mindate descending -> "", 2025-03-01, 2024-01-15, 2023-06-01, 2022-01-01   (empty FIRST)
  enabled ascending  -> 0,0,0,1,1

Discriminating numeric test (rows 1/1/1/2/700 happens to sort the same way
lexically, so I forced a case where the two answers differ). I overwrote the
live DOM's data-rows with 9/10/100/2/25 and re-sorted:
    result            ["2","9","10","25","100"]
    numericWouldBe    ["2","9","10","25","100"]   matchesNumeric = true
    lexicalWouldBe    ["10","100","2","25","9"]   matchesLexical = false
data-size has no header button (the brief's sortable set is File/Rows/Date
Range/On), but it is emitted as raw bytes and the comparator has the numeric
branch for it, so the "25K vs 4K" hazard cannot arise.


=== 4. THE HIGH-RISK ITEM — SORT SURVIVES HTMX SWAPS — PASS ===

I did NOT infer the swap template from the page's include chain. I established
it from the handler code and then from the real HTTP response bodies.

(a) Which template each swap endpoint renders, from the Go source
    (`grep -rn "RenderPartial(" --include=*.go`, non-test call sites):
      internal/handlers/explorer/handlers.go:589  handleFileToggle  -> "filemanager-file-list"
      internal/handlers/explorer/handlers.go:650  handleFileUpload  -> "filemanager-file-list"
      internal/handlers/explorer/handlers.go:751  handleFileDelete  -> "filemanager-file-list"
    At HEAD all three read `RenderPartial(w, "file-list", partialData)` (HEAD
    handlers.go:583/640/740) — that was attempt 1's defect. No RenderPartial
    call site anywhere in the tree passes a dynamic partial name that could
    reach the file list (whatif/handlers.go:367 uses a variable, but it is a
    whatif-scoped name and does not touch this table).

(b) There is exactly one define of that name:
      web/templates/pages/filemanager.html:1334  {{define "filemanager-file-list"}}
    and it is the same block the initial page includes at filemanager.html:212
    (`<div id="file-list">{{template "filemanager-file-list" .}}</div>`).
    So swap markup and initial markup are literally the same template.

(c) REAL RESPONSE BODIES. Built `go build -o <scratchpad>/p13bin ./cmd/server`
    and ran it as
      BUDGET_DATA_DIR=<scratchpad>/p13data BUDGET_LISTEN_ADDR=127.0.0.1:18913
    against three fixture CSVs (one with no parseable dates). Server log:
      "Templates loaded successfully: 49 files" ... no template errors at any point.

    TOGGLE:
    $ curl -s -X POST http://127.0.0.1:18913/explorer/files/toggle \
        -d "file=zeta-bank.csv&enabled=false" -o toggle.html -w "HTTP %{http_code} bytes=%{size_download}\n"
      HTTP 200  bytes=8215
      data-sort-btn   count=4      scope="col"     count=5
      data-name=      count=3      data-size=      count=3
      data-rows=      count=3      data-mindate=   count=3
      data-enabled=   count=3      aria-hidden="true" count=4
      row attrs in the body:
        data-name="alpha-card.csv" data-size="48"  data-rows="1" data-mindate="2025-03-01" data-enabled="1"
        data-name="mid-nodate.csv" data-size="33"  data-rows="1" data-mindate=""           data-enabled="1"
        data-name="zeta-bank.csv"  data-size="76"  data-rows="2" data-mindate="2024-01-15" data-enabled="0"

    UPLOAD (multipart POST /explorer/upload):
      HTTP 200  bytes=10019   data-sort-btn=4  scope="col"=5  data-name=4  data-mindate=4

    DELETE (DELETE /explorer/files/mid-nodate.csv):
      HTTP 200  bytes=8241    data-sort-btn=4  scope="col"=5  data-name=3  data-mindate=3

    Legacy-partial markers in all three bodies: ">Delete<" = 0,
    "Transactions</th>" = 0. The identical curl the ADR recorded returning 0
    for attempt 1 now returns 4.

(d) Re-apply logic lives OUTSIDE the swapped subtree. In the rendered page,
    `<div id="file-list">` starts at byte offset 15823 and its `</table>`
    closes at 24088; `window.FileManagerSort` is defined at 37862 and the
    `document.body.addEventListener('htmx:afterSettle', ...)` is at 41636 —
    both well after the swap target closes (script-after-table = true).
    innerHTML swaps into #file-list therefore cannot destroy them.
    Page has hx-boost occurrences: 0, so document.body is never replaced.

(e) END-TO-END: sort state actually survives a real swap. With
    name/descending active I clicked a row's On checkbox and waited for
    htmx:afterSettle:
      clickedRow        zeta-bank.csv
      orderBeforeSwap   [zeta-bank.csv, uploaded.csv, alpha-card.csv]
      orderAfterSwap    [zeta-bank.csv, uploaded.csv, alpha-card.csv]
      ariaAfterSwap     name:descending, rows:-, mindate:-, enabled:-
      arrowAfterSwap    name="▼", others ""
      btnCountAfterSwap 4      scopeColAfterSwap 5
      tableIsSameNode   false        <-- the table WAS genuinely replaced
    Same-node = false proves the DOM was rebuilt by htmx, yet order, aria-sort
    and the indicator all persisted. This is the exact scenario attempt 1 failed.

(f) EXPLORER PAGE STILL RENDERS. The three handlers are shared with Explorer:
