=== 6. SCOPE CREEP — PASS, with one deviation recorded ===

  - No `?sort=` / `?order=` / `page=` / `limit=` / `offset=` query parameters
    anywhere in filemanager.html (grep, excluding the data-sort-* attributes:
    no matches). Sorting is entirely client-side.
  - No pagination, no filtering added.
  - No default sort order imposed: state.column starts null, initial aria-sort
    is "-" on all four headers, and the observed initial row order
    [alpha-card, big25k, mid-nodate, uploaded, zeta-bank] is exactly the
    server's GetFileInfo/Glob order, untouched until a header is clicked.
  - GetFileInfo is unmodified (loader.go untouched; not in the diff).

  DEVIATION, flagged for the lead rather than failed:
  The brief said "do not change any Go handler" (Scope) and checker note 6 said
  "No Go handler changed". The worker changed three handlers —
  handlers.go:589/650/751, each a one-token change of the RenderPartial
  template name from "file-list" to "filemanager-file-list", plus explanatory
  comments — and deleted the orphaned define. The full Go diff is +16/-5 with
  no logic, routing, data or signature change (verified by reading the whole
  diff), and internal/http/http.go and every other RenderPartial call site are
  untouched. I do not score this as scope creep because it is the minimal fix
  to the exact defect the lead's own ADR names: the ADR calls
  `RenderPartial(w, "file-list", ...)` at handlers.go:583 "the P13
  sort-survival defect", and attempt 2's acceptance criterion (the swap body
  must carry the sort markup) is unreachable without either this change or
  duplicating the whole sortable table into explorer.html, which would leave
  two copies to drift. It is nonetheless a literal departure from the written
  brief and the lead should confirm the ruling.


